WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 102: The Portsmouth Peace Conference of 1905: Teddy Roosevelt's Nobel Triumph

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.760
[Rebecca] Today we visit the maritime shores of Portsmouth, New Hampshire, where in the sweltering August of 1905, the destiny of world empires hung in the balance.

4
00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:32.000
[Rebecca] The Russo-Japanese War, the first modern mechanized clash of the 20th century, had raged across Manchuria and the Tsushima Strait, leaving hundreds of thousands dead and bringing both the Russian Empire and Imperial Japan to the brink of financial and military ruin.

5
00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:42.640
[Rebecca] Enter President Theodore Roosevelt, who boldly stepped into the global arena as an honest broker, inviting the exhausted combatants to the cool ocean breezes of the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard.

6
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:57.840
[Rebecca] Through round-the-clock telephone calls, personal appeals to the Tsar and Emperor, and sheer bulldog tenacity, Roosevelt hammered out the historic Treaty of Portsmouth, catapulting America into the vanguard of world diplomacy and earning himself the Nobel Peace Prize.

7
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.360
[Rebecca] Here is George with the full fascinating story.

8
00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:15.680
[George] In February 1904, the simmering imperial rivalry between the Russian Empire and the Empire of Japan exploded into open warfare over dominant control of Manchuria and the Korean peninsula.

9
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:29.920
[George] To the astonishment of European powers who viewed Asian nations through racist lenses of colonial superiority, Imperial Japan struck first, launching a surprise-night torpedo attack on the Russian naval fleet anchored at Port Arthur.

10
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:38.080
[George] What followed was an unprecedented preview of the horrific mechanized warfare that would engulf the world a decade later in World War I.

11
00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:53.200
[George] At the siege of Port Arthur, Russian defenders armed with rapid-fire maxim machine guns and barbed wire mowed down wave after wave of advancing Japanese infantry, leaving over 100,000 soldiers dead or wounded in bloody trenches.

12
00:01:53.200 --> 00:02:05.000
[George] In March 1905, at the Battle of Mukten, the largest land battle in human history up to that point, over 600,000 troops clashed across a 50-mile front in freezing weather.

13
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:17.480
[George] Two months later, at the Battle of Tsushima, Admiral Hihakiro Togo's modern battleships annihilated Russia's Baltic fleet in just 24 hours, sinking or capturing 21 warships.

14
00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:22.280
[George] Yet, despite stunning tactical victories, Japan was bleeding to death.

15
00:02:22.280 --> 00:02:32.440
[George] Its military treasury was completely exhausted, its army had suffered over 80,000 battle deaths, and foreign bankers refused to issue further war loans.

16
00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:38.840
[George] Russia, meanwhile, was racked by revolutionary chaos following the bloody Sunday massacre in St.

17
00:02:38.840 --> 00:02:44.520
[George] Petersburg, with strikes and peasant mutinies threatening to topple Tsar Nicholas II.

18
00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:51.400
[George] Both combatants were desperate for peace, but neither could afford the domestic humiliation of suing for it first.

19
00:02:51.400 --> 00:03:03.960
[George] European military observers stationed with both armies were stunned by the horrific lethality of modern artillery shrapnel and rapid fire rifles, filing urgent tactical reports that went largely unheeded.

20
00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:09.880
[George] Observing the bloodbath from the White House was 46-year-old President Theodore Roosevelt.

21
00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:22.600
[George] Roosevelt was a visionary grand strategist who understood that a total Russian collapse would leave Japan as an unchecked hegemon in the Pacific, threatening American trade interests and the open door policy in China.

22
00:03:22.600 --> 00:03:31.160
[George] Conversely, a prolonged war of attrition could trigger a chaotic social revolution across Russia, destabilizing European security.

23
00:03:31.160 --> 00:03:42.280
[George] In June 1905, following private backchannel pleas from Japanese leaders and quiet consent from the beleaguered Tsar, Roosevelt took a breathtaking diplomatic gamble.

24
00:03:42.280 --> 00:03:49.840
[George] He issued a formal public invitation to both imperial governments to send plenipotentiary peace envoys to the United States.

25
00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:57.440
[George] It was the first time in history that a non-European nation was asked to arbitrate a catastrophic war between great world powers.

26
00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:08.800
[George] Roosevelt initially considered hosting the negotiations in Washington, D.C., but the sweltering, humid summer heat of the capital in July and August was notoriously oppressive.

27
00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:24.000
[George] Seeking a secure, dignified, and comfortably climate-controlled sanctuary where foreign diplomats could negotiate away from the prying eyes of yellow journalists and political demonstrators, the Navy department recommended the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard.

28
00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:31.120
[George] Situated on Seavies Island in the Piscatakwa River between Kittery, Maine, and Portsmouth, New Hampshire.

29
00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:43.520
[George] Roosevelt recognized that unchecked Japanese naval supremacy in the Western Pacific could destabilize global commercial shipping lanes and threaten vulnerable American territories in the Philippines and Hawaii.

30
00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:51.120
[George] In early August 1905, the opposing peace delegations arrived on the American coast aboard naval cruisers.

31
00:04:51.120 --> 00:05:05.200
[George] Representing the Russian Empire was Count Sergei Witt, a towering, brilliant former finance minister whose sharp political intellect was matched by an imposing 6'6 frame, accompanied by Ambassador Baron Roman Rosen.

32
00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:21.120
[George] Representing Imperial Japan was Harvard educated foreign minister Baron Kimura Jutaro and Ambassador Takahira Kagoro, refined and quiet diplomats who bore the immense psychological weight of a proud empire that felt entitled to the spoils of total victory.

33
00:05:21.120 --> 00:05:29.520
[George] On August 5, Roosevelt hosted a formal diplomatic luncheon aboard the presidential yacht Mayflower in Oyster Bay, Long Island.

34
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:41.960
[George] In a masterpiece of protocol design, Roosevelt avoided embarrassing questions of diplomatic precedence by arranging a round luncheon table with no head or foot, famously raising a toast.

35
00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:49.080
[George] I drink to the welfare and prosperity of the sovereigns and the peoples of the two great nations whose representatives have met here.

36
00:05:49.080 --> 00:06:01.400
[George] The two delegations then sailed up the Atlantic coast to Portsmouth, arriving on August 8 amidst roaring cannon salutes from harbor forts and cheering crowds of local citizens lining the riverbanks.

37
00:06:01.400 --> 00:06:11.400
[George] The delegates were housed at the luxurious Wentworth by the Sea Hotel in Newcastle, commuting daily to the naval shipyard aboard steam launches under naval escort.

38
00:06:11.400 --> 00:06:20.840
[George] New Hampshire citizens embraced the international visitors with overwhelming hospitality, offering buggy rides, lobster dinners and garden parties.

39
00:06:20.840 --> 00:06:32.600
[George] Count Witt deliberately courted American journalists and the local New Hampshire public, charming seaside crowds with grandfatherly warmth and turning American public sentiment in Russia's favor.

40
00:06:32.600 --> 00:06:41.160
[George] Formal negotiations commenced on August 9 inside the General Stores building, Building 86, at the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard.

41
00:06:41.160 --> 00:06:53.560
[George] In a cool second-floor conference room fitted with electric fans and telegraph lines, the delegates sat across a green bay's table, with English and French serving as the common diplomatic languages.

42
00:06:53.560 --> 00:07:05.720
[George] Initially, rapid progress was made on eight of the twelve proposed treaty articles, with Russia agreeing to recognize Japan's paramount interests in Korea and evacuate military forces from Manchuria.

43
00:07:05.720 --> 00:07:26.560
[George] But on August 18, the conference slammed into a seemingly insurmountable brick wall over two non-negotiable Japanese demands, a massive cash war indemnity of 1.2 billion yen to reimburse Japan for the cost of the war, and the total annexation of the resource rich island of Sakhalin off the Siberian coast.

44
00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:30.560
[George] Count Witt delivered an unyielding ultimatum from Tsar Nicholas.

45
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.520
[George] Russia had lost battles on foreign soil, but its heartland was unconquered.

46
00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:43.920
[George] Not a single copic would be paid as tribute or indemnity to a foreign power, and not an inch of historic Russian territory would be surrendered.

47
00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:52.800
[George] Baron Kimura responded with icy firmness, stating that Japan's dead soldiers demanded financial compensation and territorial reward.

48
00:07:52.800 --> 00:08:01.200
[George] By August 25, after days of bitter deadlock, Witt ordered his secretaries to pack their trunks and book steamship tickets back to Europe.

49
00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:05.920
[George] The Portsmouth Conference was on the verge of total, catastrophic collapse.

50
00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:17.120
[George] Tsar Nicholas insisted that paying financial indemnity was the badge of a conquered vassal state, vowing to fight on the Siberian frontier for years rather than surrender imperial honor.

51
00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:27.120
[George] Refusing to allow the conference to fail, Theodore Roosevelt intervened with legendary energy and diplomatic finesse from his summer home at Sagamore Hill.

52
00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:39.040
[George] Operating via long distance telephone, telegraph cables, and private emissaries, Roosevelt initiated an extraordinary campaign of personal backchannel diplomacy with world monarchs.

53
00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:53.520
[George] Roosevelt bypassed the diplomats in Portsmouth completely, sending urgent personal cables directly to Kaiser Wilhelm II of Germany, urging him to use his familial influence with his cousin, Tsar Nicholas, to save the peace.

54
00:08:53.520 --> 00:09:10.520
[George] Simultaneously, Roosevelt sent direct, confidential messages to the Japanese elder statesmen in Tokyo, bluntly warning them that world public opinion would turn violently against Japan if they restarted a bloody war merely to extort cash tribute from an impoverished rival.

55
00:09:10.520 --> 00:09:13.640
[George] Roosevelt proposed an ingenious compromise.

56
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:26.200
[George] Russia would cede the southern half of Sakhalin Island below the 50th parallel, which had historically been settled by Japanese fishermen, while Japan would completely drop its demand for any monetary war indemnity.

57
00:09:26.200 --> 00:09:29.160
[George] Roosevelt's strategic pressure bore fruit.

58
00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:29.720
[George] In St.

59
00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:43.320
[George] Petersburg, Tsar Nicholas reluctantly consented to partition Sakhalin, while in Tokyo, Emperor Meiji and his cabinet overruled their hawkish military generals and ordered Baron Kamura to accept the American compromise.

60
00:09:43.320 --> 00:09:56.280
[George] Roosevelt's tireless personal mediation proved that diplomatic arbitration conducted by a neutral, trusted superpower could break even the most intractable geopolitical deadlocks between proud warring empires.

61
00:09:56.280 --> 00:10:07.560
[George] On the morning of Tuesday, August 29, 1905, delegates assembled at the naval shipyard for what everyone expected to be the final, bitter adjournment of the conference.

62
00:10:07.560 --> 00:10:14.360
[George] Journalists from around the globe paced nervously outside the building, expecting war to resume within hours.

63
00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:29.240
[George] Inside the conference room, Baron Kamura rose from his chair, looked Count Witt in the eye, and quietly announced that Imperial Japan consented to withdraw its demand for war reimbursement entirely and would accept the southern half of Sakhalin.

64
00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:33.960
[George] Witt remained silent for a long moment, then nodded in solemn agreement.

65
00:10:33.960 --> 00:10:35.400
[George] Peace was made.

66
00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.200
[George] Spontaneous gasps echoed through the room.

67
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:44.920
[George] Secretaries rushed to the windows to flash secret handkerchief signals to telegraph operators waiting in the yard below.

68
00:10:44.960 --> 00:10:56.960
[George] On September 5, 1905, the formal Treaty of Portsmouth was officially signed inside Building 86, concluding 30 articles of peace in parallel English and French texts.

69
00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:12.000
[George] As the delegates signed the parchment, church bells rang out across Portsmouth, naval cannons fired a 21-gun salute across the harbor, and citizens danced in the streets celebrating the end of a war that had claimed over 150,000 lives.

70
00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:23.360
[George] The signing of the Treaty of Portsmouth silenced the artillery across Manchuria, allowing hundreds of thousands of exhausted peasant conscripts to return home to their families in Russia and Japan.

71
00:11:23.360 --> 00:11:27.920
[George] The Treaty of Portsmouth was a transformative watershed in world history.

72
00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:39.840
[George] For his brilliant, tireless arbitration, Theodore Roosevelt was awarded the 1906 Nobel Peace Prize, becoming the first American in history to receive the prestigious international award.

73
00:11:39.840 --> 00:11:47.920
[George] Roosevelt donated the entire $40,000 cash prize to the United States Congress to fund industrial labor research.

74
00:11:47.920 --> 00:11:54.400
[George] Yet the aftermath of Portsmouth revealed the fraught, dangerous complexities of modern geopolitical diplomacy.

75
00:11:54.400 --> 00:11:56.560
[George] While Count Witt returned to St.

76
00:11:56.800 --> 00:12:08.320
[George] Petersburg celebrated as a diplomatic hero for preserving Russian territory without paying tribute, Baron Kimura returned to Tokyo to face violent, deadly anti-government riots.

77
00:12:08.320 --> 00:12:20.320
[George] Japanese citizens, who had sacrificed sons and endured crushing war taxes, were outraged by the absence of a cash indemnity, burning police stations and tram cars in the Hibiah riots.

78
00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:27.680
[George] The lingering public resentment planted deep seeds of Japanese anti-Americanism that would fester over the coming decades.

79
00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:40.600
[George] Nevertheless, the Portsmouth Peace Conference marked the definitive arrival of the United States as a premier global superpower, capable of shaping the destiny of nations through moral prestige and diplomatic resolve.

80
00:12:40.600 --> 00:12:50.520
[George] Today, at the Portsmouth Naval Shipyard and the Wentworth by the Sea Hotel, historic commemorative plaques and original conference tables remain preserved.

81
00:12:50.520 --> 00:13:05.720
[George] The Treaty of Portsmouth stands as an eternal testament to the extraordinary power of quiet diplomacy, reminding the world that even the bloodiest and most intractable conflicts can be resolved when courage, patience, and statesmanship unite across the table.

82
00:13:05.720 --> 00:13:19.320
[George] The Nobel Peace Prize awarded to Theodore Roosevelt cemented the United States' emergence from 19th century isolationism onto the center stage of 20th century global statesmanship and international law.

83
00:13:19.640 --> 00:13:30.760
[Rebecca] The Treaty of Portsmouth ended the first mechanized clash of the 20th century, winning Theodore Roosevelt the Nobel Peace Prize and launching America onto the world diplomatic stage.

84
00:13:30.760 --> 00:13:32.200
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

85
00:13:32.200 --> 00:13:39.160
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we ascend Mount Washington for the story of the greatest surface wind ever measured.

